{
  "version": 1,
  "summary": "The evidence secret scan finds tokens after any encoded byte and JSON-escaped tokens in every file, and names evidence it cannot read instead of stopping.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "internal/runner/evidence.go",
      "summary": "Owns the token boundary rule, the decoded second pattern pass, and the Scan result with its two causes.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            26,
            29
          ],
          "summary": "tokenStart accepts a token after a repeated URL escape, a quoted-printable byte, or any backslash escape form.",
          "rationale": "Each of these encodings ends in a letter or digit, so the old lookbehind treated the token as glued to an identifier and the run went green (PX-3b). Six cases in TestDishonestWordResultsAreInconclusive fail on main and pass here. A token glued to plain ASCII letters still does not count, by design.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            55,
            74
          ],
          "summary": "Scan holds the first leak and the first unreadable entry; Cause gives the one reason text for words and frames.",
          "rationale": "Evidence verilex cannot read may hold a secret, so it must never pass as clean, but the word did run, so 'not run' was wrong (PX-12). Frame embeds Scan, so run.json gains an optional 'unread' field beside 'leak' and old records still load.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            122,
            159
          ],
          "summary": "The walk names an entry it cannot open, skips it, and keeps scanning the rest of the evidence.",
          "rationale": "Before, the first permission error stopped the walk, so a token in a later file stayed in evidence. A directory that cannot be listed is visited a second time with the error, which is why the name and rename logic runs only when err is nil. The chmod of a closed directory is recorded only after it succeeds, because a failed one restored in the defer would turn the scan into an error. Proven by TestUnreadableEvidenceAnotherUserOwns and a verify-verilex drive with unshare.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            369,
            384
          ],
          "summary": "redact runs every pattern a second time on the JSON-decoded text and maps each match back to the escapes it spans.",
          "rationale": "A token written as ghp\\u005f... was found only in a report that decodes whole, so other files and broken reports kept it (PX-8, PX-11). The decoded text reuses the memory of the raw one, so peak memory stays at main's level; time roughly doubles for files that contain a backslash (50 MB escape-heavy log: 1.2 s to 2.7 s). The raw pass stays because project patterns may match escapes literally.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            404,
            468
          ],
          "summary": "decode and unescapeAt turn JSON escapes into runes exactly as encoding/json does, including UTF-16 pairs and lone surrogates.",
          "rationale": "A first version had a nine-byte escape table against an eight-rune replacement and panicked on \\t; the verify-verilex perf drive caught it. TestPatternsMatchDecodedJSONEscapes now covers every escape, a pair and a lone surrogate, and two mutations of this code make it fail.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/runner/runner.go",
      "summary": "Words, frames and cleanup take their scan reason from Scan.Cause.",
