          "summary": "The launch's decoded-value check now only catches what the raw and decoded scans cannot, such as an anchored project pattern.",
          "rationale": "The frame scan now finds an escaped token itself, so this branch no longer runs for that case. TestSecretOnlyDecodingShowsReplacesWholeStdout keeps the branch covered with the pattern ^hunter2$.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "tests/run_test.go",
      "summary": "Adds a failing-on-main test per defect and one shared probe for files another user owns.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            834,
            857
          ],
          "summary": "otherUser probes a real chown to a subordinate uid and checks the new owner before a foreign-owner test runs.",
          "rationale": "On Ubuntu 24.04, AppArmor lets unshare start but denies chown inside it, so the old probe (unshare ... true) passed and the tests ran without any foreign file. The GitHub Linux runner fails instead of skipping, so CI cannot pass these tests vacuously. Verified with a fake unshare: skip locally, fail with GITHUB_ACTIONS=true, and fail when chown leaves the owner unchanged.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": ".github/workflows/ci.yml",
      "summary": "Grants unshare the user namespace on the Linux runner, as bwrap already had.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            58,
            61
          ],
          "summary": "The sandbox step writes the same unconfined AppArmor profile for unshare as for bwrap.",
          "rationale": "Without it, chown inside the namespace fails, so the PX-12 tests failed on CI and the older foreign-owner tests passed without testing anything. The test job passed on the PR with this step.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-secret-scan-followups"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
